    <p>Q2. If <code>outbuf->dropped</code> is non-zero, what happened?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="dropped counts missed USB samples, not decoding errors. CRC failures are tracked separately in stats.">The Mode S CRC failed on this buffer</li>
      <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. dropped is incremented in rtlsdrCallback() when free_bufs == 0 (the ring buffer was full). The decoder thread wasn't consuming buffers fast enough, so the reader had to discard incoming samples.">The ring buffer was full when this block arrived; samples were discarded</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The bounce buffer is a Pi-specific workaround for kernel zero-copy issues, not related to the dropped counter.">The bounce buffer was used on a Raspberry Pi</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Trailing samples are valid overlap from the previous buffer, deliberately copied. They don't set dropped.">The trailing samples from the previous buffer were invalid</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="3">
    <p>Q3. <code>wakeDecode()</code> is called inside the lock in the happy path, but also called <em>outside</em> the lock when the FIFO is full. Why is it safe to call outside the lock?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="pthread_cond_signal is not inherently safe without a mutex — but the specific use here is safe because of the reasoning below.">pthread_cond_signal is always lock-free</li>
      <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. When the FIFO is full, calling wakeDecode() without the lock is safe because the goal is just to make sure the decoder isn't sleeping — a spurious wakeup is harmless. The decoder re-checks its condition (buffer available?) under its own lock anyway. The POSIX condition variable model allows signaling outside the mutex for this pattern.">A spurious wakeup is harmless — the decoder re-checks its condition under its own lock</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no atomic flag here. wakeDecode() calls pthread_cond_signal, not an atomic store.">It uses an atomic store which bypasses mutex requirements</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The reader thread is the one calling wakeDecode() — it signals the decode thread, not itself.">The decode thread signals itself</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

