<h2>Check Your Understanding</h2>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Scenario A:</strong> You add a <code>fprintf</code> inside the main conversion loop in <code>convert_uc8_nodc()</code>. The program immediately grinds to a halt. Why?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleA" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansA" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
<code>convert_uc8_nodc()</code> is called once per USB transfer (~35 times/second) and processes ~32,000 samples per call. A <code>fprintf</code> per sample = ~1.1 million writes to stderr per second — each involving a syscall, locking the stderr buffer, and flushing. This instantly overwhelms the process. The right approach is to add the print <em>outside</em> the sample loop (once per buffer) or use antiSpam to rate-limit to once per N callbacks.
</div>
</div>

<div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
<strong>Scenario B:</strong> <code>mean_level</code> is consistently 0.03 (very low). What does this tell you, and what should you check?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleB" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansB" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
A <code>mean_level</code> of 0.03 means the average magnitude is only 3% of full scale — the signal is very quiet. Possible causes: (1) gain is set too low; (2) antenna is disconnected or poorly connected; (3) SDR is tuned to the wrong frequency; (4) the converter is producing invalid output (bug). Start with <code>rtlsdr_set_tuner_gain()</code> — check <code>RTLSDR.curGain</code> or enable the gain debug output. Then verify the frequency (<code>Modes.freq</code>). A totally absent signal (antenna unplugged) will produce near-zero mean_level.
</div>
</div>

